00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.370 align:start position:0%
 
for today's classic interview we're  gonna get into the topic of climate  science climate change the environment  pollution fossil fuels and all of that  stuff and we're gonna go to my interview  with Richard Heinberg back from July of  2015 because in the midst of all this  craziness that we see in terms of  science denial from the current  administration in the midst of the Paris  climate agreement and the climate talks  in Poland which we spoke about last week  and everything that we're seeing from  the Trump administration which is  actually going in the opposite direction  when it comes to the climate there's  like a practical question that has to be  addressed when we talk about getting off  of fossil fuels which is oh is that  actually possible right now what would  be the logistics can we generate the  amount of energy that we currently use  from alternative sources yes or no and  then also on that basis do we need to be  using as much energy as we're currently  using there's there's sort of multiple  layers to this and that's exactly where  my conversation with Richard Heinberg  focused so that's this week's classic  interview think about what he told me  when I interviewed him a couple of years  ago in the context of what we currently  hear from the climate science deniers on  the right both in terms of the logistics  of getting off of fossil fuels but also  the economic prosperity that the deniers  wrongly think is inextricably linked to  fossil fuel harvesting consumption and  the effects thereof let's take a listen  I'm joined today by Richard Heinberg  who's senior fellow of the post carbon  Institute also author of 12 books most  recently after burn society beyond  fossil fuels and I found the book so  interesting because we talk sometimes  abstractly about how we have the  technology to move beyond fossil fuels  now but we're not always getting the  analysis of can we really do that what  would it actually take to fully  transition our society off of fossil  fuels and as you write it's not  necessarily the easiest thing is it  right well it's going to be an enormous  challenge first of all it is  is inevitable and it's necessary if  we're going to deal with climate change  and the fact of the declining quality of  the fossil fuels that were still able to  get out of the ground the transition to  renewable energy is necessary and  inevitable but it ain't going to be easy  the problem is that these energy sources  solar and wind provide different kinds  of energy from what we're accustomed to  using they produce electricity yes and  we use electricity for a lot of things  but only about 20% of final energy is  consumed as well as electricity we  consume all the other energy in lots of  different forms liquid fuels for  transportation coal and natural gas to  produce industrial levels of heat for  making cement and steel and and solar  panels and wind turbines for that matter  I mean the way we produce these  renewable energy technologies today is  itself almost entirely dependent upon  fossil fuels so making that transition  is going to be a challenging process and  we've only really stuck our toe in the  water up to this point I mean right now  renewable energies are mostly in the  electricity sector and even there in  most countries they're producing only a  small amount of total energy is it not a  sort of stepladder approach in the sense  that you know as a simple example we  could say oK we've got an automobile  whoo an automobile that depends on  gasoline we transition that automobile  to run on electricity instead of  gasoline but in the short term that  electricity may well be generated by  coal for example so we're just sort of  taking a step up that ladder the next  step of course would be to get that  electricity from a renewable source and  it seems that this is where you're sort  of pointing out we are we are going to  run up against the first challenge which  is that well that that next step how do  we get off of generating that  alternative energy how do we produce the  solar panels for example without still  using fossil fuels is that is that a  sore  of analogy to what you're saying right  yes and then the next step after that is  how do we build the car using renewable  energy all that steel where does the  plastic come from the tires currently  the synthetic rubber that is used to  make tires is made out of oil why don't  we run that car on in terms of road  surfaces because currently we build our  roads from asphalt there are potential  ways of solving these problems we can  produce industrial heat using  electricity which could be made from  solar panels and wind turbines but  currently doing it that way would cost  about three times as much so the glass  the steel the cement the asphalt all of  these basic industrial materials are  likely to be much more expensive in an  all renewable energy world so this sort  of ties into the other issue you've  written about which is connected to  something we've been discussing which is  that you argue that broadly speaking  this sort of status quo globalized  consumer society dependent on fossil  fuels as you've just described is not  really sustainable long term anyway talk  about that a little bit well we're we  extract all kinds of materials from the  Earth's crust and we use fossil fuels to  do that of course fossil fuels are some  of the materials that we extract but  also minerals and metals of all kinds  and you know if you think about it even  for a few seconds this is inherently an  unsustainable enterprise you know you  can't continue extracting non-renewable  resources at ever increasing rates and  do that forever at a certain point we  bump up against limits to growth and of  course there's been a very robust  discussion about limits to growth going  on since the 1970s it appears that we  are in fact hitting those limits to  growth this this century and we're doing  it at all kinds of ways in  energy terms with depleting fossil fuels  and with the problems we've just been  talking about about the with regard to  the replacements for fossil fuels with  regard to water with regard to metals  and other non renewable materials we  extract from from the earth with regard  to biodiversity really the only solution  from a long-term standpoint to all of  these problems is to reduce the scale of  the human enterprise within the overall  ecosystem and we haven't been willing to  talk about that up to this point we it's  all it's all been about growth growing  human numbers and growing per capita  human consumption well you can do that  for a while but ultimately we do live on  a finite planet and it's about time we  opened the discussion about you know how  big an enterprise can we make this the  human enterprise within the the limits  that are out there and then how do we  adapt to those limits once there with  insight and the systemic change you're  describing really would require a pretty  significant paradigm shift just in terms  of how we see our society and the role  of humans within it which is almost  weird to say right because it's humans  have created the Society and we're at a  point where we need to kind of explore  whether the current role of humans  within that society makes sense and I'm  curious what you think about the  increased automation that seems to be on  the horizon over the next 20 to 30 years  whether that might prove to be a good  catalyst for this discussion in this  particular sense often when we talk  about autonomous vehicles and the impact  that that could have on transportation  and on so many industries the reflexive  reaction is it's going to kill jobs  period and then when I say well  historically the argument that jobs will  disappear has never held back  technological progress so let's for a  second assume the progress will happen  maybe we need to be revisiting whether  this idea of work for a living period  that exists in human society is the most  logical path forward because technology  will lead to more  Meishan and i'm curious whether you  think that may serve as a good  jumping-off point to re-examine the sort  of place of humans within society right  well you know automation will certainly  occur at certain levels but we're also  talking about a situation where overall  energy supplies are likely to shrink and  that means that that we're going to have  to replace that energy with something  and in many cases I think it's going to  be human labour ironically even as we  automate a lot of the more  intellectually oriented activities using  computer technology some of the more  sort of you know basic work that we  thought had been automated a long time  ago like agricultural work may begin to  require more and more human labour and  in some ways that may not be a bad thing  we need things for people to do  obviously but also you know as we  distance ourselves from the natural  world using automation we become we  extract ourselves from the only context  that really matters which is the context  of the global ecosystem and we stop  thinking like an ecosystem and we start  thinking like automatons and that can be  a kind of deadly thing if it's carried  to its extreme so more people growing  more food locally is I think one of the  one of the features of an all renewable  society and and that's that's almost  unquestionably our future in the last  bit of time we have left I'd be curious  to hear from you if we want to be  proactive about this rather than just  react to possibly diminishing energy  resources or whatever whatever a  reactive sort of path we want to take  you mentioned the growth of local food  the cultivation of local food what other  proactive things can we do well you know  we're going to have to start rethinking  all the ways that we use energy and for  example  we've been talking about metals and  minerals well we're going to have to  recycle almost all the materials that  we're using much more thoroughly than we  currently are and of course the  electricity sector which is is fairly  obvious increasing the scope of  renewable energy within the electricity  sectors as quickly as possible  transportation is going to be one of the  more difficult sectors to transition and  so we're gonna have to start thinking  about a less mobile future what does it  mean when you know it's just not so  easily affordable to hop on an airplane  and travel from one continent to another  shipping again it's going to be a  difficult transition so localizing  basically what we're talking about is  real okeh lysing economies turning  globalization on its head and learning  how to live with a lot less  all right certainly a lot of people do  not like the sound of that living with  less is never one of the the corporate  media narratives around the future it  just doesn't seem to play too well with  all of the advertisers and all of the  the players that are influencing the  news we get doesn't your right well I  just came back from Greece spent about  15 days in Athens and other other places  in Greece and folks there are learning  to live with less and it's it's not  always easy but in many ways I think  Greece is the future of the rest of  industrial society there is sort of the  canary in the coal mine so we should be  wishing those folks well in their  transition and also learning from them  as they adapt to life with less energy  and less stuff all right the book is  after burn society beyond fossil fuels  we've been speaking with Richard  Heinberg who's a senior fellow of the  post carbon Institute thanks so much for  joining me it's been a pleasure David  thank you

